{"author":{"id":"694fb8161bd8bf91df2a28db7c649e48f8c5fa47ad3ad0234607d35f0482801a"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1789375505,"nonce":"jfcp0P1PgppDVSpgu2jFa8TiDEM=","title":"A constructor call reads the JIT state seven times","message":"`call_constructor_sync` rooted the instance and each argument through\n`push_jit_root`, read them back through `jit_root_at`, copied the context\nwith `read_jit_ctx`, swapped it with `mutate_jit_ctx`, entered the body\nthrough `call_jit_fn_pub` (whose `ensure_ctx_reg` reads the state again),\nrestored it with `set_jit_context`, and truncated the roots with\n`jit_roots_restore_len`: each an out-of-line thread-local lookup on macOS,\nseven or more per construct, plus a 128-byte null fill of the argument\narray. It also looked the callee module up by name (96426b5).\n\nFix: the JIT path fetches `jit_state` once and works through it, as\n`wren_call_inner` does since 69d5fe7 — roots pushed and read on the\nstate's vector, the context saved, written and restored in place, and\nthe body entered through `call_jit_at(j, ..)`, a public form of\n`call_jit_cached_st`. The argument array is written only as far as used.\nThe interpreter fallback is unchanged, in `call_constructor_sync_impl`.","files":null}]}